1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:25,160
The highly radioactive core vessel of homogeneous reactor experiment number two,

2
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:31,160
known as HRE2, lies out of sight 20 feet below the thick concrete blocks

3
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:32,679
on which these men are standing.

4
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:41,240
They work under the most restricted conditions, at times with a periscope providing limited visibility,

5
00:00:41,879 --> 00:00:44,700
at other times with no view of the work below.

6
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:52,240
The men are manipulating tools that reach down into the core vessel.

7
00:00:53,179 --> 00:00:58,539
They are repairing and modifying the vessel to improve the reactor's operational behavior.

8
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:06,640
Homogeneous Reactor Experiment No. 2 began its nuclear operation in this building

9
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:09,900
at Oak Ridge National Laboratory in December 1957.

10
00:01:14,659 --> 00:01:20,359
This cutaway model of the building shows the reactor in its cell below ground level.

11
00:01:20,359 --> 00:01:27,599
operation of hre2 provides information regarding the feasibility of homogeneous power reactors

12
00:01:27,599 --> 00:01:37,599
fueled with an aqueous solution hre2 was designed as a five megawatt two region reactor consisting

13
00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:45,560
of core and blanket systems a pump continuously circulates a dilute heavy water solution of

14
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:51,740
urinal sulfate, copper sulfate, and sulfuric acid, through the core where

15
00:01:51,740 --> 00:01:57,120
fissioning occurs, and heat is generated at temperatures up to 300 degrees

16
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:04,060
centigrade. Past a pressurizer, which provides system pressures up to 2,000

17
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:09,939
pounds per square inch, through a heat exchanger where heat is released and

18
00:02:09,939 --> 00:02:13,699
and steam is produced to drive the turbine generator.

19
00:02:16,099 --> 00:02:20,979
The fuel circulates back through the core vessel at a rate of 400 gallons per minute.

20
00:02:24,419 --> 00:02:29,219
Uranium in the fuel solution is highly enriched in the 2,35 isotope.

21
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:35,620
The dissolved copper sulfate is a catalyst or recombination of radiolitic gas.

22
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:49,099
The blanket system contains a pump, a pressurizer, and a heat exchanger similar to those in the

23
00:02:49,099 --> 00:02:54,780
fuel system.

24
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:59,219
Blanket fluid circulates in the space between the core vessel and the pressure vessel at

25
00:02:59,219 --> 00:03:02,560
a rate of 230 gallons per minute.

26
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:09,800
This fluid is heavy water, which serves as a neutron reflector.

27
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:16,000
The welded core vessel, the first large Zircaloy vessel ever made, was an experiment in both

28
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,120
fabrication and application.

29
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:27,140
The conical inlet section contains nine perforated plates to diffuse the solution and provide

30
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:33,520
slug flow through the sphere.

31
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:40,740
The reactor was operating at design conditions of 5 megawatts when, in April 1958, a hole

32
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:50,560
formed in the core vessel and allowed fuel solution to transfer to the blanket system.

33
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:55,380
Reactor operating procedures were revised to permit operation with fuel in both the

34
00:03:55,379 --> 00:04:02,019
the core and blanket systems. Without changes in equipment or piping, the operation continued

35
00:04:02,019 --> 00:04:11,120
for two years. Six long runs, the last for 105 days of continuous operation, were made

36
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:18,259
in accomplishing most of the original objectives. At the end of this period, a second hole formed

37
00:04:18,259 --> 00:04:21,779
in the core vessel.

38
00:04:21,779 --> 00:04:27,420
After formation of the first hole, considerable remote viewing was done inside the vessel

39
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:32,579
while the reactor was shut down between runs.

40
00:04:32,579 --> 00:04:37,959
It was found that several diffuser plates were loose, but the hole was below the top

41
00:04:37,959 --> 00:04:43,680
five plates and could not be viewed from inside the core.

42
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:47,340
By use of this special viewing device,

43
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:50,340
lowered into the blanket region,

44
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:54,720
the outside wall of the core was inspected to determine whether the hole originated

45
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:58,120
from a weld failure, a metal defect,

46
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:00,120
or from some other cause.

47
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:07,579
This is the hole as seen in the mirror of the blanket viewing device.

48
00:05:07,579 --> 00:05:12,259
It was concluded that the failure was probably due to a melt through.

49
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:23,879
Further full-scale hydrodynamic tests were made to verify the mechanism whereby such

50
00:05:23,879 --> 00:05:30,379
a melt through might occur and to develop an improved core flow pattern.

51
00:05:30,379 --> 00:05:35,120
When small metal chips and sand particles were introduced into this mock up of the core

52
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:41,519
vessel, it was determined that there were local regions of flow separation and low velocity

53
00:05:41,519 --> 00:05:47,859
along the wall of the diffuser cone on the upper diffuser plates and along the wall of

54
00:05:47,859 --> 00:05:51,399
the lower portion of the hemisphere.

55
00:05:51,399 --> 00:05:56,219
Uranium absorbing scale might accumulate in these regions and increase the temperature

56
00:05:56,219 --> 00:06:01,759
of the fluid to a point where boiling and chemical phase changes could cause further

57
00:06:01,759 --> 00:06:09,079
uranium deposition and melting of the diffuser plates and the core wall.

58
00:06:09,079 --> 00:06:13,800
When some of the plates were loosened, as in the reactor, they were floated by hydraulic

59
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:21,000
forces and created non-uniform flow distribution.

60
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:28,360
Particulate material still accumulated along the wall when the flow direction was reversed.

61
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:33,479
With plates removed from the 90-degree cone and with flow in the normal direction, there

62
00:06:33,479 --> 00:06:38,860
were still areas where scale-like particles could accumulate.

63
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:44,800
with the flow reversed and the plates removed, the particulate material did not collect along

64
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:51,900
the wall. Although the flow distribution is not ideal, turbulence in the vessel should

65
00:06:51,900 --> 00:07:00,180
provide acceptable cooling of the wall. Any corrosion scale on the wall should be removed

66
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:06,800
at low solution flow velocities. Combined results of hydrodynamic studies, operation

67
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:13,800
of the reactor and visual observations led to certain conclusions. It was decided to

68
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:22,900
modify and repair the core vessel. The top five diffuser plates would be removed. Corrosion

69
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:32,060
products and uranium bearing scale would be cleaned from the core wall. Wall thickness

70
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:40,939
thickness would be measured ultrasonically. A specimen from the wall in the region of the

71
00:07:40,939 --> 00:07:47,519
second hole would be removed for metallographic examination to verify that the holes had resulted

72
00:07:47,519 --> 00:07:58,439
from melting of the wall. The hole would then be taper reamed to prepare it for plugging. Both

73
00:07:58,439 --> 00:08:05,800
Both holes would be patched.

74
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:13,160
And the direction of flow would be reversed so that fuel solution would enter at the top.

75
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:17,899
This work had to be done under extremely restricted conditions.

76
00:08:17,899 --> 00:08:26,279
The only access to the vessel assembly is through small flanged openings.

77
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:33,180
The blanket access is three and a half inches in diameter, and the core access is only two

78
00:08:33,180 --> 00:08:35,680
and an eighth inches.

79
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:42,980
The radiation level in the empty vessel is greater than 100,000 rontgens per hour, permitting

80
00:08:42,980 --> 00:08:48,580
approach only through five foot thick concrete shielding plugs.

81
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:54,759
These plugs are 20 feet above the lower portion of the vessel.

82
00:08:54,759 --> 00:09:00,019
In the working area, airflow must be carefully controlled because much of the contamination

83
00:09:00,019 --> 00:09:02,559
is loose particulate matter.

84
00:09:02,559 --> 00:09:11,700
Furthermore, the intensely radioactive particles contaminate any tools placed in the vessel.

85
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:17,480
To accomplish the objectives under these conditions, special devices and tools were designed and

86
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:20,240
built.

87
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:26,039
And in this dimensionally accurate mock-up, the devices were thoroughly tested and programmed

88
00:09:26,039 --> 00:09:30,960
before use in the reactor.

89
00:09:30,960 --> 00:09:35,840
Devices were developed for viewing and inspecting both the inside and the outside of the core

90
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:37,399
vessel.

91
00:09:37,399 --> 00:09:44,620
For viewing within the core vessel, a periscope seven-eighths of an inch in outside diameter

92
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:50,659
and 21 feet long was developed by Lerma Engineering Corporation.

93
00:09:50,659 --> 00:09:55,060
It contains cerium-stabilized radiation resistant lenses

94
00:09:55,060 --> 00:09:59,220
and is equipped with interchangeable objective elements.

95
00:09:59,220 --> 00:10:04,019
The lenses provide a 180 degree field of vision straight down

96
00:10:04,019 --> 00:10:09,519
or a 30 degree field at a right angle.

97
00:10:09,519 --> 00:10:15,180
Detailed examination of the vessel as well as assistance with some of the operations,

98
00:10:15,180 --> 00:10:19,240
was provided by this instrument.

99
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:23,740
Lighting was supplied separately by 500 watt projector lamps

100
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:28,580
caged and mounted on 1⁄4 inch tubing.

101
00:10:28,580 --> 00:10:34,520
In some instances, viewing was essential, but room in the 2 and 1⁄8 inch opening

102
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:38,340
could not be spared for lights and a periscope.

103
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:43,740
A device was therefore developed at Oak Ridge National Laboratory, which incorporated a

104
00:10:43,740 --> 00:10:49,580
zirconium oxide arc lamp and lens system to project a beam of light through a hole in

105
00:10:49,580 --> 00:10:53,240
a mirror to the area to be viewed.

106
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:59,740
The reflected image is viewed in the mirror with a monocular.

107
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:04,940
Several operations inside the core were guided by viewing devices which permitted looking

108
00:11:04,940 --> 00:11:07,960
into the holes from the blanket side.

109
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:13,440
The blanket viewing device, shown being tested in the mock-up, had to enter the blanket vessel

110
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:16,680
through a 3 1⁄2-inch pipe.

111
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:21,840
Then it had to reach behind the core vessel with a horizontal 30-inch arm carrying 200

112
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:25,120
watts of lighting and a scanning mirror.

113
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:31,160
The device has a monocular and fixed mirror at the top with a fixed mirror and a rotating

114
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:35,720
mirror at the bottom.

115
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:39,800
articulated manipulator carrying a miniature television camera was

116
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:45,000
developed. The device has three hydraulically operated elbows and one

117
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:52,279
wrist and can be programmed to any spot on the core vessel. Manipulators were

118
00:11:52,279 --> 00:11:57,220
made to accurately position ultrasonic transducers for measuring the thickness

119
00:11:57,220 --> 00:12:04,240
of the spherical and conical sections of the core wall. Measurements made during

120
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:09,600
the life of the reactor along with original fabrication data permit

121
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:17,500
periodic determination of corrosion rates working tools for each operation

122
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:24,519
were designed built and thoroughly tested in a mock-up the first mechanical

123
00:12:24,519 --> 00:12:28,960
operation was to have been the cutting of diffuser plates free from the vessel

124
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:36,259
wall by use of milling cutters and saw blade segments these tools were not used

125
00:12:36,259 --> 00:12:43,960
in the reactor because the plates were found to be already loose the plates

126
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:50,960
range from 10 to 22 inches in diameter since the access is only two and an

127
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:57,300
eighth inches in diameter this miniature heliar cutting torch and manipulator

128
00:12:57,300 --> 00:13:03,780
were devised to cut the plates into narrow strips.

129
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:09,940
A protective angle iron supports the fragile manipulator during handling.

130
00:13:09,940 --> 00:13:17,440
The angle iron is removed and the torch is lowered into position in the mock-up.

131
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:24,120
The operator with no view of his work lowers the manipulator hook to pick up the top plate.

132
00:13:24,120 --> 00:13:36,159
plate, 22 inches in diameter, is raised, is lowered over the ground electrode clamp, falls

133
00:13:36,159 --> 00:13:46,159
into position, and is latched. Cuts are then made as simulated here. Gauges assure that

134
00:13:46,159 --> 00:13:53,539
each strip will be one and a half inches wide. A check run is made after each cut to guarantee

135
00:13:53,539 --> 00:14:01,500
that the cut was complete. The operator controls the torch by instruments, the width of the

136
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:10,779
cut by gauges and the length of the cut by a travel indicating scale. The actual cutting

137
00:14:10,779 --> 00:14:17,579
is done underwater and the procedure was thoroughly demonstrated at the test station. At the reactor

138
00:14:17,580 --> 00:14:21,139
In eight hours, three plates were lifted, latched, and cut.

139
00:14:30,379 --> 00:14:36,480
The radioactive strips are fished from the vessel with the aid of the projected light viewer.

140
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:41,640
Five plates were cut, and the strips were fished from the core vessel in two weeks.

141
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:44,960
The strips were deposited in a carrier above the core access

142
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:52,820
flange and below the shielding plugs. A variety of articulated tools were built for removing

143
00:14:52,820 --> 00:15:01,019
objects from the vessel. The core was cleaned and descaled with an ordinary wire brush driven

144
00:15:01,019 --> 00:15:07,740
by a small air motor. The brush and motor are mounted on a manipulator, which presses

145
00:15:07,740 --> 00:15:13,600
the brush against the core wall.

146
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:18,399
Consecutive overlapping passes are made over the spherical and 90 degree conical portion

147
00:15:18,399 --> 00:15:20,960
of the vessel.

148
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:26,379
An impression device obtained exact dimensional definition of both holes.

149
00:15:26,379 --> 00:15:31,320
Each impression was made by a thermoplastic block electrically heated and then driven

150
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:35,120
into the hole by an air cylinder.

151
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:39,399
device is lowered into the core vessel mock up to previously calibrated points.

152
00:15:39,399 --> 00:15:44,240
The arm is raised and the operator searches for the lower hole with the tip

153
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:50,740
of the impression device. His only view is through the blanket viewing device.

154
00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:57,460
When the operator finds the hole, he extends the air cylinder and drives the

155
00:15:57,460 --> 00:16:07,379
block. Satisfied with the alignment, he withdraws it, heats the plastic, drives it into place,

156
00:16:07,840 --> 00:16:14,780
permits it to cool, and removes it from the hole. This is a thermoplastic block before use,

157
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:21,580
one that has been driven into the lower hole, a clean replica of the contaminated master,

158
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:25,460
and a zircaloy patch for the lower hole,

159
00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:28,960
contour milled using the plastic master.

160
00:16:30,220 --> 00:16:35,639
A replica of the upper hole was made to provide the pilot for this specimen removal saw.

161
00:16:35,820 --> 00:16:42,720
This tool had a two-fold purpose, to obtain a specimen for metallographic examination

162
00:16:42,720 --> 00:16:47,540
and to perform one step in the preparation of the hole for plugging.

163
00:16:47,540 --> 00:16:57,780
This is a photograph of the upper hole as formed and this picture shows the hole after brittle

164
00:16:57,780 --> 00:17:06,660
scale had been chipped away. The circle indicates the area to be removed by the saw. The pilot for

165
00:17:06,660 --> 00:17:14,420
the saw had to fit in the hole and provide the front radial bearing. The saw shown in the

166
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:23,460
developmental stage has an erecting mechanism a light a periscope a means for rotating the saw

167
00:17:25,460 --> 00:17:32,340
a cylinder for advancing and withdrawing it and a device for retaining the specimen

168
00:17:33,220 --> 00:17:40,900
the tool successfully retrieved a specimen for examination and created a round hole

169
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:46,140
as shown by this photograph.

170
00:17:46,140 --> 00:17:51,040
This photograph shows the hole after it was reamed to a conical surface by a tool similar

171
00:17:51,040 --> 00:17:56,500
to the specimen removal saw.

172
00:17:56,500 --> 00:18:00,460
The patch placement tool performed the final operations.

173
00:18:00,460 --> 00:18:08,180
The upper end has the erecting mechanism.

174
00:18:08,180 --> 00:18:30,640
the toggle nut placement mechanism, the bolt torquing drive, and the bolt release.

175
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:36,980
At the lower end, mounted for insertion, is the patch with gold gasket and the toggle

176
00:18:36,980 --> 00:18:43,240
bolt assembly in the closed position. The tool is erected, the toggle is inserted through

177
00:18:43,240 --> 00:18:51,019
the hole, is opened and pulled back against the wall of the vessel for tightening. With

178
00:18:51,019 --> 00:18:56,180
the patch insertion tool in position in the mockup, the operator looks into the blanket

179
00:18:56,180 --> 00:19:05,220
viewer and aligns the end of the toggle bolt with the hole. The bolt is inserted, it springs

180
00:19:05,220 --> 00:19:08,680
is pulled back and tightened.

181
00:19:11,240 --> 00:19:16,720
This is the patch for the lower hole as it appears in place in the vessel mock-up

182
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:18,980
and in the reactor.

183
00:19:24,420 --> 00:19:27,539
Since the upper hole is in a region of greater heating

184
00:19:27,539 --> 00:19:33,220
and lower flow velocity, it is desirable that the patch be set in the tapered hole

185
00:19:33,220 --> 00:19:36,160
so as to be flush with the vessel wall.

186
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:41,299
The zircaloy bolt has a flush countersunk head

187
00:19:41,299 --> 00:19:45,539
with a hexagonal wrenching head of nickel brazed to it.

188
00:19:46,819 --> 00:19:50,220
The head will dissolve during pre-critical operations,

189
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:53,539
leaving a flush patch and bolt assembly.

190
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:58,039
Here is the patch in place in the reactor vessel

191
00:19:58,039 --> 00:20:02,200
before operation of the reactor has dissolved the bolt head.

192
00:20:03,220 --> 00:20:13,779
Because the tools became highly contaminated in use, special procedures and precautions were

193
00:20:13,779 --> 00:20:20,920
required to protect the personnel and prevent the spread of particulate contamination. Each

194
00:20:20,920 --> 00:20:28,539
tool after having served its purpose had to be withdrawn and disposed of. Contaminated

195
00:20:28,539 --> 00:20:33,519
components of HRE 2 had been replaced routinely throughout the reactor's

196
00:20:33,519 --> 00:20:40,119
lifetime then beginning in March 1960 work began on repairing and modifying

197
00:20:40,119 --> 00:20:47,799
the very heart of the reactor throughout the work there was not a single case of

198
00:20:47,799 --> 00:20:55,480
overexposure of personnel to radiation and by August 1960 the last tool had

199
00:20:55,480 --> 00:21:03,360
been removed from the core. Despite the radiation hazard, the necessity that only remote tools be

200
00:21:03,360 --> 00:21:11,099
used, the limited visibility, and in some cases total lack of visibility, extremely complex

201
00:21:11,099 --> 00:21:18,740
mechanical operations were performed in the irradiated core vessel. Thus, the practicality

202
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:25,099
of extremely difficult and totally unanticipated maintenance of circulating fuel reactors

203
00:21:25,100 --> 00:21:26,380
was demonstrated.

204
00:21:27,320 --> 00:21:31,000
The reactor resumed nuclear operation at low power

205
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:35,900
with flow reversed through the core on November 7, 1960.

206
00:21:36,820 --> 00:21:39,840
The degree of improvement in the reactor's behavior

207
00:21:39,840 --> 00:21:42,980
will be revealed in continued operation

208
00:21:42,980 --> 00:21:45,260
with the remodeled core.
